      "format": "essay",
      "difficulty": "medium",
      "context": "You are studying the effect of temperature on the rate of product formation for a newly discovered human digestive enzyme, lactase-X. You perform an assay measuring the amount of product formed per minute at various temperatures. The data show that the reaction rate increases steadily from 10 °C to 37 °C, reaches a maximum rate at 37 °C, and then drops sharply to near zero at 60 °C.",
      "question": "Based on the experimental context provided, complete the following tasks: (a) Describe the molecular interactions responsible for maintaining the enzyme's active site structure at 37 °C. (b) Explain the change in reaction rate as the temperature increases from 10 °C to 37 °C. (c) Explain why the reaction rate drops sharply at 60 °C, referencing the impact on the enzyme's structure.",
      "rubric": [
        {
          "criterion": "Part (a)",
          "description": "Describes noncovalent interactions (such as hydrogen bonds, ionic bonds, and hydrophobic interactions) that stabilize the tertiary structure and active site of the enzyme.",
          "points": 1
        },
        {
          "criterion": "Part (b)",
          "description": "Explains that increasing temperature increases kinetic energy and molecular collisions, thereby increasing the frequency of successful enzyme-substrate interactions up to the optimum.",
          "points": 1
        },
        {
          "criterion": "Part (c)",
          "description": "Explains that temperatures of 60 °C disrupt the noncovalent interactions holding the tertiary structure together, causing denaturation and a permanent loss of the active site shape.",
          "points": 1
        }
      ],
      "keyPoints": [
        "Noncovalent bonds maintain tertiary structure.",
        "Kinetic energy increases collision frequency at lower temperatures.",
        "High temperatures cause denaturation and alter the active site."
      ],
      "sampleOutline": "(a) Hydrogen bonds, ionic bonds, and hydrophobic interactions maintain the specific folded conformation of the active site. (b) As temperature rises toward 37 °C, molecules move faster, increasing the kinetic energy and the rate of collisions between the enzyme and substrate. (c) At 60 °C, thermal agitation exceeds the strength of the stabilizing bonds, causing the enzyme to denature and lose its functional shape."
    }
  ]
}

